    "title": "Pediatric Asthma Management Protocol",
    "domain": "Asthma",
    "role": "Nurse Care Manager",
    "implementation_guidance": "This enhanced protocol integrates the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research (CFIR) to guide its successful adoption, implementation, and sustainment. Key considerations include: 1. Assess the protocol's characteristics (Innovation Domain), such as its evidence base, adaptability, and cost. 2. Evaluate the external environment (Outer Setting), including local attitudes, policies, and partnerships. 3. Analyze the organizational context (Inner Setting), focusing on structural characteristics, culture, and readiness for implementation. 4. Consider the individuals involved (Individuals Domain), their knowledge, self-efficacy, and stage of change. 5. Follow a structured implementation process (Process Domain) involving planning, engaging stakeholders, executing, and reflecting.",
    "expected_outcomes": "Enhanced outcomes include a higher percentage of patients achieving well-controlled asthma (measured by ACT/GINA scores with 95% CIs), reduced asthma exacerbation rates (ED visits and hospitalizations per 100 patient-years with 95% CIs), improved asthma-specific quality of life (PAQLQ scores with 95% CIs), and a reduction in asthma-related school absenteeism (days per patient per year with 95% CIs). The Number Needed to Treat (NNT) will be calculated for key interventions to prevent one exacerbation or hospitalization.",
    "target_population": "The target population is pediatric patients with asthma, with a specific focus on addressing the needs of racial and ethnic minority children and those from low-income backgrounds who are disproportionately affected by asthma. The protocol is designed to be culturally adapted to the diverse backgrounds of patients and their families, including language accessibility and consideration of traditional health beliefs.",
    "evidence": "The protocol is grounded in a comprehensive evidence base, including the NAEPP guidelines. Evidence supports the efficacy of inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) in reducing exacerbations and improving lung function (specific effect sizes to be added from literature). Research also demonstrates that personalized written asthma action plans reduce hospitalizations (specific effect sizes to be added from literature). Environmental control interventions targeting indoor allergens have been shown to reduce asthma symptoms and medication use (specific effect sizes to be added from literature). Culturally tailored self-management education programs, such as the CALMA intervention, have demonstrated significant improvements in asthma control in specific populations [3]. The use of the CFIR is supported by implementation science research [4].",
    "health_equity": "This protocol prioritizes health equity by addressing the needs of racial and ethnic minority children and those from low-income backgrounds. It incorporates strategies for cultural adaptation, including language accessibility, consideration of traditional health beliefs, and a family-centered approach. The protocol explicitly addresses disparities by systematically screening for and addressing Social Determinants of Health (SDOH), improving access to care through telehealth and flexible scheduling, tailoring education to diverse literacy levels, and training providers to mitigate systemic bias.",
    "measurement": "Process indicators: Percentage of nurse care managers adhering to the protocol, patient education completion rates, SDOH screening rates, follow-up appointment completion, and medication adherence. Outcome measures: Percentage of patients with well-controlled asthma (with 95% CIs), asthma exacerbation rates (ED visits and hospitalizations per 100 patient-years with 95% CIs), asthma-specific quality of life scores (with 95% CIs), and school absenteeism rates (with 95% CIs). Implementation metrics: Adoption rate, fidelity to the protocol, reach of the intervention, cost-effectiveness, sustainability, acceptability, and feasibility."
