      {
        "title": "Health Equity and Cultural Adaptation for PTSD Screening in Medicaid Populations",
        "content": [
          {
            "subtitle": "Strategies to Promote Mental Health Equity in Medicaid Managed Care",
            "details": {
              "summary": "Medicaid programs play a crucial role in promoting mental health equity, as they serve a disproportionate number of people of color and individuals with low incomes, who often face significant barriers to accessing mental healthcare. Structural racism and a fragmented mental healthcare system contribute to disparities in access, diagnosis, treatment, and outcomes.",
              "strategies": [
                {
                  "strategy": "Direct Quality Improvement Strategies",
                  "description": "States can require MMC entities to identify mental health disparities and implement interventions to promote equity. This includes stratifying mental healthcare measures by race and ethnicity, engaging enrollees in the design of quality improvement initiatives, and holding MCEs accountable for performance through public reporting and financial incentives."
                },
                {
                  "strategy": "Facilitate Access to Services",
                  "description": "States can direct MCEs to cover a broader range of clinical and non-clinical mental healthcare services, such as crisis stabilization, peer support, and community health workers. This includes facilitating partnerships between MCEs and community-based organizations with trusted relationships in their communities."
                },
                {
                  "strategy": "Integrate Mental Health in Primary Care",
                  "description": "Integrating mental healthcare into primary care settings can improve access and reduce stigma. States can require MCEs to support integrated care models and implement payment models that ensure sufficient reimbursement for these services."
                },
                {
                  "strategy": "Address Workforce Diversity",
                  "description": "A racially and ethnically diverse mental health workforce is essential for providing culturally competent care. States can require MCEs to implement strategies to recruit and retain a diverse workforce, such as offering loan repayment programs, scholarships, and training opportunities."
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "subtitle": "Cultural Adaptation of PTSD Screening and Treatment",
            "details": {
              "summary": "Culturally considerate trauma-focused PTSD treatment is essential for improving outcomes in diverse populations. Key considerations include:",
              "considerations": [
                {
                  "consideration": "Cultural Humility and Training",
                  "description": "Training in cultural humility and the impact of racism on healthcare is critical for all providers."
                },
                {
                  "consideration": "Community-Based and Whole-Person Care",
                  "description": "Access to services in community settings and a focus on whole-person care, including addressing social conditions, can improve engagement and outcomes."
                },
                {
                  "consideration": "Culturally Adapted Interventions",
                  "description": "Interventions should be adapted to be culturally relevant and responsive to the specific needs of different populations. This may involve incorporating cultural values, beliefs, and practices into treatment models."
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Measurement Frameworks for PTSD Screening and Implementation",
        "content": [
          {
            "subtitle": "Primary Care PTSD Screen for DSM-5 (PC-PTSD-5)",
            "details": {
              "summary": "The PC-PTSD-5 is a concise, 5-item screening tool designed to identify individuals with probable PTSD in primary care settings. A positive screen indicates the need for further assessment, ideally with a structured interview.",
              "keyFeatures": [
                {
                  "feature": "Trauma Exposure Assessment",
                  "description": "The screen begins by asking about exposure to traumatic events. If no trauma history is reported, the screen is complete with a score of 0."
                },
                {
                  "feature": "Symptom Assessment",
                  "description": "If trauma exposure is indicated, respondents answer five yes/no questions about how the trauma has affected them in the past month."
                },
                {
                  "feature": "Scoring",
                  "description": "A score of 0-5 is generated based on the number of \"yes\" responses. A cut-point of 4 is generally used to indicate elevated risk, balancing false negatives and false positives. However, practitioners may consider a lower cut-point for women due to higher false negatives at a cut-point of 4, or a higher cut-point if resources are limited."
                },
                {
                  "feature": "Sample Items",
                  "description": "Questions cover common PTSD symptoms such as nightmares or unwanted thoughts, avoidance of reminders, being constantly on guard, feeling numb or detached, and guilt or self-blame related to the event."
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "subtitle": "Hospital Mental Health Risk Screen (HMHRS)",
            "details": {
              "summary": "The HMHRS is a 10-item self-report screen developed to predict mental health problems after a recent trauma, particularly in hospitalized patients. It has shown good performance in predicting posttraumatic stress, depression, and anxiety symptoms two months post-trauma across diverse ethnoracial groups and language speakers (English and Spanish).",
              "keyFeatures": [
                {
                  "feature": "Target Population",
                  "description": "Developed for use with patients hospitalized after emergency care for injury or acute illness."
                },
                {
                  "feature": "Administration",
                  "description": "A self-report measure that can be completed in under 3 minutes."
                },
                {
                  "feature": "Scoring",
                  "description": "Item responses are summed for a total score. Scores of 10 or more indicate an elevated risk for later mental health problems."
                },
                {
                  "feature": "Cultural Relevance",
                  "description": "Evaluated in diverse populations, including Asian, American Pacific Islander, Black, Latino/a, multi-race, and White individuals, and in both English and Spanish speakers."
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "subtitle": "Trauma Screening Questionnaire (TSQ)",
            "details": {
              "summary": "The TSQ is a 10-item symptom screen designed for use with survivors of all types of traumatic stress. It is based on items from the PTSD Symptom Scale - Self Report (PSS-SR) and includes five re-experiencing items and five arousal items.",
              "keyFeatures": [
                {
                  "feature": "Symptom Endorsement",
                  "description": "Respondents indicate whether they have experienced specific symptoms at least twice in the past week."
                },
                {
                  "feature": "Scoring",
                  "description": "A screen is considered \"positive\" when at least six items are endorsed. It is recommended that screening be conducted three to four weeks post-trauma to allow for normal recovery processes."
                },
                {
                  "feature": "Referral",
                  "description": "Those screening positive should be assessed with a structured interview for PTSD."
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      }
