      {
        "title": "CFIR Domains for Implementation Strategy",
        "content": "The Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research (CFIR) provides a comprehensive typology of constructs that can influence implementation effectiveness. The following domains and selected constructs are critical for guiding the implementation of post-disaster mental health support [8]:",
        "domains": [
          {
            "name": "Innovation Domain",
            "description": "This domain focuses on the characteristics of the intervention itself.",
            "constructs": [
              "Innovation Evidence-Base: The extent to which the intervention has robust evidence supporting its effectiveness (e.g., PFA, TF-CBT) [8].",
              "Innovation Adaptability: The degree to which the intervention can be modified, tailored, or refined to fit local context or needs, especially for diverse cultural groups [8].",
              "Innovation Complexity: The perceived difficulty of implementing the intervention, which can impact training and resource allocation [8].",
              "Innovation Cost: The affordability of the intervention's purchase and operating costs, crucial for resource-constrained post-disaster environments [8]."
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "Outer Setting Domain",
            "description": "This domain encompasses the external environment influencing the implementation.",
            "constructs": [
              "Critical Incidents: Large-scale and/or unanticipated events that disrupt implementation and/or delivery of the innovation, directly relevant to disaster contexts [8].",
              "Local Attitudes: Sociocultural values and beliefs that encourage support for implementation, particularly important for community engagement and cultural adaptation [8].",
              "Local Conditions: Economic, environmental, political, and technological conditions enabling support for implementation [8].",
              "Policies & Laws: Legislation, regulations, and guidelines that support implementation, such as Medicaid policies for mental health services [8].",
              "Financing: Availability of external funding (e.g., grants, reimbursement) to implement and deliver the innovation, crucial for sustained support [8]."
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "Inner Setting Domain",
            "description": "This domain pertains to the characteristics of the implementing organization or setting.",
            "constructs": [
              "Structural Characteristics: Infrastructure components (physical, IT, work) that support functional performance [8].",
              "Relational Connections: Quality of formal and informal relationships within and across the setting [8].",
              "Culture: Shared values, beliefs, and norms, including human equality-centeredness, recipient-centeredness, deliverer-centeredness, and learning-centeredness, which are vital for a supportive implementation environment [8].",
              "Tension for Change: The perception that the current situation is intolerable and needs to change, often high post-disaster [8].",
              "Compatibility: The fit of the innovation with existing workflows, systems, and processes [8].",
              "Available Resources: Availability of funding, space, materials, and equipment for implementation [8]."
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "Individuals Domain",
            "description": "This domain focuses on the characteristics of individuals involved in the implementation and delivery of the intervention.",
            "constructs": [
              "Roles: Clear definition of roles for high-level leaders, mid-level leaders, opinion leaders, implementation facilitators, leads, team members, deliverers, and recipients [8].",
              "Characteristics: Individual capabilities (knowledge, skills), opportunities (availability, power), and motivation (commitment) to fulfill their roles effectively [8]."
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "Implementation Process Domain",
            "description": "This domain describes the activities and strategies used to implement the innovation.",
            "constructs": [
              "Teaming: Intentional coordination and collaboration on interdependent tasks to implement the innovation [8].",
              "Assessing Needs: Regularly assessing the needs of the affected population and the implementing setting [8].",
              "Planning: Developing a detailed plan for implementation, including strategies for addressing barriers and leveraging facilitators [8].",
              "Executing: Carrying out the implementation plan, adapting as necessary based on ongoing monitoring [8].",
              "Reflecting & Evaluating: Regularly reviewing progress, evaluating outcomes, and using data for continuous improvement [8]."
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Conclusion",
        "content": "This protocol provides a framework for developing and implementing post-disaster mental health support that is evidence-based, culturally sensitive, and equity-focused. By systematically addressing the various domains of implementation and utilizing robust measurement strategies, communities can enhance their resilience and recovery in the face of future disasters."
      }
